{"question": "A 56-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of nausea and shortness of breath that started while he was at work one hour ago. He also reports lightheadedness that started earlier in the day. He has type 2 diabetes mellitus, for which he takes metformin and sitagliptin. He has smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 20 years. He appears pale and diaphoretic. A 12-lead ECG is shown. Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of this patient's symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Viral myocarditis", "B": "Acute pericarditis", "C": "Acute inferior myocardial infarction", "D": "Saddle pulmonary embolus", "E": "Second-degree AV block"}, "pred_answer": "C", "gold_answer": "C", "question_domains": ["Emergency Medicine", "Cardiology", "Endocrinology", "Pulmonology", "Internal Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Cardiology", "Pulmonology"], "question_analyses": {"Emergency Medicine": "The patient's symptoms of nausea, shortness of breath, and lightheadedness, combined with his medical history of type 2 diabetes mellitus and smoking, along with his appearance (pale and diaphoretic), suggest a serious cardiovascular event, most likely an acute coronary syndrome (ACS), such as an ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) or non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI). The 12-lead ECG would be crucial in confirming the diagnosis, but based on the symptoms and risk factors, ACS is the most likely cause. ### Key Aspects of the Issue:\n1. **Symptoms and Onset**: The sudden onset of symptoms while the patient was at work, particularly nausea and shortness of breath, are concerning. These symptoms are often indicative of cardiac ischemia or infarction. 2. **Medical History**: The patient has type 2 diabetes mellitus and a history of smoking, both of which are significant risk factors for coronary artery disease (CAD) and ACS. 3. **Physical Examination**: The patient appears pale and diaphoretic, which are signs of hypoperfusion and can be indicati